.TH CREATESIGNERKEY 8
.SH NAME
createsignerkey, mkauthinfo \- create signer key on authentication server, and keys signed by it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B auth/createsignerkey
[
.BI -a " alg"
] [
.B -c
] [
.BI -f " keyfile"
] [
.BI -e " expiry"
] [
.BI -b " bitsize"
]
.I name
.PP
.B auth/mkauthinfo
[
.BI -k " keyspec"
] [
.BI -e " expiry"
]
.I user
[
.I keyfile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Createsignerkey
creates public and private keys that are used by a server acting as `signer' to generate certificates for users.
.I Name
appears as signer in each certificate.
The
.I expiry
date has the form
.IR ddmmyyyy ,
is converted to seconds since the epoch
(see
.IR daytime (2))
and stored in the
.IR keyfile ;
the certificate is good until the last second of that day, local time.
A date that is not one (month 00, 31 February), one already past,
and a year from 2038 on, which an expiry time cannot hold, are refused.
By default the server's certificate never expires.
.PP
For the algorithms that take a size, the key will be
.I bitsize
long (default: 2048 bits) with a minimum of 2048 bits and a maximum of 4096 bits;
.B -b
with an algorithm whose keys have one size is refused.
(The minimum was once 32 bits, and a 32-bit signer's own certificate did not verify.)
.I Keyfile
is the file in which the server stores its keys;
the default is
.BR /keydb/signerkey ,
and many authentication programs such as
.IR logind (8)
by default expect to find their server key there.
Creating a signer's default key afresh typically invalidates all certificates previously issued by that signer,
because their signatures will not verify.
The
.I keyfile
holds the signer's secret key and
should be readable only by the user running
those programs:
a new file is made mode 600, and a file that is already there
has the permissions of other users taken from it before the key is written
(if they cannot be,
.I createsignerkey
writes nothing).
.PP
The
.B -a
option specifies the signature algorithm, one of
.BR ed25519
(the default),
.BR mldsa65 ,
.BR mldsa87 ,
.BR slhdsa192s ,
.BR slhdsa256s ,
.BR rsa ,
.B elgamal
or
.BR dsa .
.PP
The
.B -c
option, or the environment variable
.B cnsamode
set, is CNSA 2.0 mode, and the mode is a rule for the key made, not a default:
the key is
.BR mldsa87 ,
its certificate is hashed with SHA-384, and the Diffie-Hellman modulus written
with it is RFC 3526's 3072-bit group (2048 bits otherwise).
In the mode an
.B -a
naming any other algorithm, before or after
.BR -c ,
and any
.BR -b ,
are refused, saying why, and nothing is written.
(The mode once only chose the algorithm when none was given:
.B "-a rsa -b 512"
made a 512-bit RSA signer in it.)
.PP
.I Mkauthinfo
makes a key for
.I user
like the signer's and a certificate for it signed by the key
.I keyspec
names (default
.BR key=default ,
see
.IR security-auth (2)),
good until the end of the day
.I expiry
or without limit, and writes both to
.I keyfile
(default: standard output) in the form of
.IR keytext (6),
mode 600.
In CNSA mode
.I mkauthinfo
signs only with a key file that is CNSA's whole
(ML-DSA-87 keys, a certificate under SHA-384 or SHA-512, a modulus of 3072 bits or more)
and says what is wrong with one that is not.
.PP
An
.B elgamal
signer key made by an earlier version is no longer accepted
(a certificate could be forged from its public half: see
.IR keyring-gensk (2)),
and neither are the keys users were given under it:
make the signer's key again and have the users enrol again.
.SH FILES
.B /keydb/signerkey
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/auth/createsignerkey.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/auth/mkauthinfo.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR security-auth (2),
.IR keyring-gensk (2),
.IR logind (8),
.IR signer (8)
